theory RHTNRecovery
/*
================================================================================
  RHTN recovery adoption -- symbolic analysis (Tamarin)
================================================================================

WHAT THIS FILE IS
  design Section 9.1 makes recovery a TWO-FACTOR act: rotating to a new key
  requires BOTH (a) a proof from the predecessor key -- the old identity
  authorising a change to itself -- AND (b) recognition by a prior
  counterparty (a verifier who has met the subject).  Neither alone
  suffices -- wire Section 4.1 states it: "Key alone is not recovery",
  because a thief holds the key too, and "presence alone is not recovery".

  The review plan's Stage 1.1 target: "A thief holding the key cannot
  produce a rotation that OUTWEIGHS the legitimate holder's."

  **ONLY HALF OF THAT TARGET IS THIS THEORY'S.**  What is proved here is the
  ADMISSION structure: a recovery a patron accepts requires both factors, so
  a thief who has stolen only the key and is not recognised by an honest
  verifier cannot complete one.  "Outweighs" is a COMPARATIVE claim about two
  successors, and design Section 9.0.2 is explicit that competing successors
  can both be valid, with observers routing to whichever attester they trust
  -- so weight is per-observer policy (design Section 16.1) settled in the
  flow metric, not a property of one acceptance.  This theory contains no
  competing successor, no standing and no ordering, and closing the quoted
  target needs both halves.

  What it does close is the half design Section 18.3 rests on: recovery holds
  against a stolen key alone, and fails only to a colluding or deceived
  verifier.

  What this model does NOT cover, deliberately: the COLLUDING-counterparty
  case (design Section 18.3), where the thief supplies factor b through a
  dishonest verifier.  That is an accepted risk, not a defended one -- the
  design's answer there is the flow-metric bound on the successor's
  standing, which the flow simulator covers, not this symbolic model.  The
  lemma below carves out compromised verifiers explicitly so the boundary
  of the claim is visible in the statement.

  Reading conventions are in attach.spthy; only new idioms are re-explained.
================================================================================
*/

begin

builtins: signing

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  PARTIES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Patron    -- countersigns the recovery adoption (design Section 9.1).
    Subject   -- the identity being recovered; holds an OLD key and wants a
                 NEW one.
    Verifier  -- a prior counterparty who can attest recognition (factor b).
  Each has a long-term signing key registered as in the other theories.
*/

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  HYBRID, BUT NOT UNIFORMLY -- and the asymmetry is deliberate
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  `wire-format.md` makes recovery's long-lived evidence hybrid and says
  exactly which parts and why:

    FIELD 9, the verifier's `match`, is "an untagged detached COSE_Sign
      carrying one Ed25519 and one ML-DSA-65 entry" -- because "field 9 forges
      a VERIFIER'S attestation, which is the attack", and its protection "is
      permanent".
    FIELD 3, the old key's successor statement, "is a COSE_Sign, not a
      COSE_Sign1 -- the old identity is hybrid, so it contributes two entries
      like any other logical signer".
    FIELD 7 STAYS CLASSICAL, "and the reason is not cost": it is "signed by
      the very key an attacker mounting a fraudulent recovery already
      controls -- hybridising it protects nothing."

  So the two rules that produce archive-retained evidence sign twice, and
  nothing else does.  Hybridising uniformly would have been easier and would
  have misrepresented a decision the design took on purpose.
*/

rule Register:
    [ Fr(~skc), Fr(~skp) ]
  --[ Reg($A), OnlyOnce($A) ]->
    [ !LtkC($A, ~skc), !LtkP($A, ~skp)
    , !Pk($A, pk(~skc))                // the classical half, for field 7
    , !PkC($A, pk(~skc)), !PkP($A, pk(~skp))
    , Out(<pk(~skc), pk(~skp)>) ]

// Explicit key theft, stamped in the trace, as in attach.spthy.  This is
// how the "thief holding the key" of the plan's target enters the model.
rule Compromise_Classical:
    [ !LtkC($A, ~skc) ]
  --[ Compromised($A), CompromisedClassical($A) ]->
    [ Out(~skc) ]

rule Compromise_PostQuantum:
    [ !LtkP($A, ~skp) ]
  --[ Compromised($A), CompromisedPQ($A) ]->
    [ Out(~skp) ]

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  FACTOR A -- the predecessor-key proof
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The old identity authorises a change to a specific new key by signing
  <'rotate', subject, newkey> with the OLD key.  Anyone holding the old key
  can produce this -- including a thief.  So this factor ALONE must not be
  enough; the lemma checks that.

  We model "the new key" as a fresh public value the subject proposes.  Its
  matching secret is not needed for the property (the property is about who
  AUTHORISED the rotation, not about using the new key afterwards), so we
  model the new key as a fresh name carried in the clear.
*/

/*
  THE OLD KEY SIGNS A THREE-WAY SUCCESSOR STATEMENT, NOT A BARE ROTATION.
  wire Section 4.1 fixes the payload as

      SuccessorStatement = [ prior_key, new_key, patron_key ]

  and requires a verifier to check new_key and patron_key against adoption
  fields 1 and 2, "an unchecked binding being the same as no binding".  The
  patron is in there for a reason: without it one old-key proof authorises a
  successor under ANY patron, so the same evidence assembles a recovery at
  P1 and again at P2.  An earlier version of this model signed
  <'rotate', S, newkey> and Tamarin found exactly that -- two patrons
  accepting one proof, no compromise anywhere.  See
  successor_statement_binds_the_patron below, which falsifies without the
  third element.
*/
/*  A LEGITIMATE VERIFIER SIGNING A RECOGNITION IT NEVER EARNED.
    ================================================================
    Its own uncompromised key, no meeting, subject and successor off the
    network.  `wire-format.md` 4.1 says the human evidence carries no
    guarantee -- a verifier "can be mistaken or lying and nothing checks it"
    -- so a wire theory that requires a meeting before V may use V's own key
    is assuming client conformity.

    Adding this rule falsified three lemmas that used to live here:
    key_alone_insufficient, recognition_names_the_meeting_key and
    recovery_requires_a_meeting.  They were not wrong, they were in the wrong
    tree, and they are now in `compliant/recovery.spthy`.  What survives here
    is what a validator can genuinely conclude -- the BINDINGS -- and those
    are below.

    NOTE WHAT DID NOT FALSIFY: recognition_binds_the_successor still holds.
    A lying verifier signs a real recognition for that successor, so
    acceptance is still attributable to it.  That is the wire's actual
    contribution and the reason a false recovery is a signed lie by a named
    party rather than an anonymous one.                                     */
rule Recognise_Without_Meeting:
    [ !LtkC($V, ~vsc), !LtkP($V, ~vsp), In(<$S, newkey>) ]
  --[ Recognised($V, $S), RecognisedFor($V, $S, newkey)
    , RecognisedWithoutMeeting($V) ]->
    [ Out(<'recognise', $S, newkey,
           <sign(<'recognise', $S, newkey>, ~vsc),
            sign(<'recognise', $S, newkey>, ~vsp)> >) ]

rule Old_Key_Proof:
    [ !LtkC($S, ~oldc), !LtkP($S, ~oldp)  // the old identity's two halves
    , In(newkey)                       // the successor -- PUBLIC once exchanged
                                       //   at the meeting, so anyone holding
                                       //   the old key can sign over it. It is
                                       //   not minted here: one keypair is
                                       //   generated and both factors must
                                       //   name THAT one, which is what makes
                                       //   the two halves of a recovery meet.
    , !Pk($P, pkP) ]                   // a REGISTERED patron, not any label
  --[ OldKeyProof($S, newkey)
    , OldKeyProofFor($S, newkey, $P) ]->
    [ Out(<'rotate', $S, newkey, $P,
           <sign(<'rotate', $S, newkey, $P>, ~oldc),
            sign(<'rotate', $S, newkey, $P>, ~oldp)> >) ]

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  FACTOR B -- recognition of the PERSON by a prior counterparty
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  design Section 9.1: recovery's human factor is a prior counterparty
  RECOGNISING THE SUBJECT.  What the verifier judges is the PERSON -- "yes,
  this is the same person I met" -- and no cryptography substitutes for that.

  WHAT THE VERIFIER SIGNS IS A DIFFERENT QUESTION, and it signs the successor
  too.  `wire-format.md` Section 4.1 requires a recovery response's subject to
  be "the newly adopted node": the verifier attests continuity TO the new key,
  so a recognition that named only the person could be assembled beside any
  successor.  See recognition_binds_the_successor.  The two facts sit together
  without tension -- the JUDGEMENT is about a face, the SIGNATURE is about
  which key that judgement licenses -- and the key it names is the one the
  meeting exchanged over the direct channel, never one supplied over the
  network (see Recovery_Meeting).

  THE OUT-OF-MODEL AXIOM.  What makes honest recognition SOUND against a
  thief is physical: the verifier checks the face (design Section 7), and a
  thief holding the victim's key does not have the victim's face.  The
  symbolic model has no term for a face, exactly as it has none for
  physical co-presence.  So `!Met($V,$S)` and the honest Recognise rule
  encode the AXIOM "an honest prior counterparty signs a recognition of S
  only for the genuine S" -- the same modelling move the ceremony model
  makes for co-presence.  The theorem below is therefore: the PROTOCOL
  STRUCTURE requires both a key proof and a human recognition; it does not
  (and cannot, symbolically) prove a look-alike is impossible.  That
  residual is design Section 18.3's colluding-counterparty case, carved out
  in the lemma as a compromised verifier.
*/

/*
  TWO DIFFERENT FACTS, AND COLLAPSING THEM IS A REAL ERROR.  Both documents
  say so, and the encoding says it most sharply.  `wire-format.md` Section
  4.1's Recovery block, field 2: "The PRESENCE half: a prior counterparty who
  MET THE SUBJECT AGAIN and recognised them."  design Section 9.1 step 1:
  "A user rotating a key MEETS, IN PERSON, someone they have met BEFORE."
  Past acquaintance AND a present encounter; only the pair licenses a
  recognition, and "again" is the word that carries it.

  An earlier version had only the past half -- a persistent !Met(V,S) that
  Recognise read forever -- so a thief holding the stolen key could obtain
  factor B from an HONEST verifier with no new meeting at all.  Tamarin found
  that trace in 9 steps, with neither verifier nor patron compromised.  The
  file called it design Section 18.3's colluding/deceived residual, which it
  was not: nobody in the trace colludes or is deceived, the rule simply fires.
*/

// PAST ACQUAINTANCE.  Persistent, because having met somebody does not stop
// being true.  This alone licenses nothing.
//
// AN ENVIRONMENTAL ASSUMPTION, NOT A DERIVED FACT.  The rule has no premises,
// so the theory may designate any pair as prior counterparties without an
// earlier presence transaction.  What is proved is therefore conditional on
// being handed a truthful !Met relation; the theory does NOT verify factor
// B's provenance from protocol history.  Deriving it would mean composing
// with ceremony.spthy -- a record accepted there licensing a !Met here --
// which no single theory in this directory does.
rule Met:
    [ ]
  --[ PriorCounterparty($V, $S) ]->
    [ !Met($V, $S) ]

/*
  THE PRESENT ENCOUNTER, and this is the model's second physical AXIOM,
  of the same kind as ceremony.spthy's co-presence: the token below stands
  for "the two of them are in a room now", the adversary has no rule that
  mints one, and the theorems are proved RELATIVE to that.

  WHAT THE AXIOM DOES NOT SAY is who the verifier is looking at.  A thief who
  physically turns up and is taken for the subject produces this same token --
  that is design Section 18.3's deceived counterparty, and it stays a
  residual.  What the axiom buys is narrower and is exactly what was missing:
  a recognition requires SOMEBODY to have shown up, so a thief working from
  the stolen key alone, remotely, cannot manufacture one.
*/
rule Recovery_Meeting:
    [ !Met($V, $S)                     // they had met before -- design §9.1
    , Fr(~nk) ]                        // the successor, exchanged AT the meeting
  --[ RecoveryMeeting($V, $S), MeetingKey($V, $S, ~nk) ]->
    [ AtMeeting($V, $S, ~nk)           // LINEAR: one recognition per meeting
    , Out(~nk) ]                       // the key is public once exchanged

/*
  THE RECOGNITION NAMES THE SUCCESSOR IT ATTESTS CONTINUITY TO.  The verifier
  still recognises the PERSON -- that is the human act -- but what it signs
  binds that recognition to the specific new key, which the subject presents
  at the meeting.  wire Section 4.1 is explicit that omitting the binding is
  a replay primitive: a proof naming no successor means "one observed proof
  would authorise an unlimited number of competing successors".  An earlier
  version signed <'recognise', S> alone, and Tamarin duly found the trace --
  one honest recognition beside two accepted recoveries for different keys.
  See recognition_binds_the_successor below, which falsifies without this.
*/
/*
  THE SUCCESSOR COMES FROM THE MEETING, NOT FROM THE NETWORK.  An earlier
  version took it from In(newkey) -- a Dolev-Yao network input, so the
  adversary chose which key an honest verifier's genuine recognition attached
  to, with neither verifier nor patron compromised.  That is an
  over-approximation: design §7.1's channel 3 is an OPTICAL channel, "QR codes
  exchanged screen-to-camera", which "carries key exchange", and a recovery
  meeting is a ceremony (§9.1).  The verifier's client reads the successor off
  the screen in front of it, so there is no network step for an adversary to
  occupy -- substituting the key means putting a device in the room, which is
  the co-presence residual §7.6 already accepts and prices, not a network
  attack.
*/
rule Recognise:
    [ !LtkC($V, ~vsc), !LtkP($V, ~vsp)  // the verifier's two halves
    , AtMeeting($V, $S, newkey) ]      // CONSUMED: meeting now, and the key
                                       //   came over the direct channel
  --[ Recognised($V, $S)
    , RecognisedFor($V, $S, newkey)
    , HonestlyRecognised($V, $S, newkey) ]->
    [ Out(<'recognise', $S, newkey,
           <sign(<'recognise', $S, newkey>, ~vsc),
            sign(<'recognise', $S, newkey>, ~vsp)> >) ]

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE PATRON ACCEPTS A RECOVERY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The patron countersigns a recovery adoption for new key `newkey` only when
  it has seen BOTH factors for that exact key:
    * a valid old-key proof (factor a), and
    * a valid recognition from a verifier it treats as a prior counterparty
      (factor b).
  Both signatures are checked (two Eq restrictions).  The patron records the
  verifier it relied on, so the lemma can ask about that verifier's honesty.
*/

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE OTHER EVIDENCE ROUTE -- A TRANSFER
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  design §6.1.1 gives an adoption two ways to carry evidence: a proof of
  presence between the two parties, or the FORMER PATRON's countersignature.
  This theory models adoptions that carry embedded evidence, so the second
  route belongs here beside the first.

  `wire-format.md` §4.1 binds the countersignature to a TransferStatement of
  [node, former_patron, new_patron], for the reason the SuccessorStatement
  binds three: a signature naming no destination would authorise an unlimited
  number of moves.  That is the same replay primitive, one field over, and the
  lemma below is the same lemma one field over.
*/

/*  THE SUBJECT COMES OFF THE NETWORK, not from `Fr`.  An earlier version
    minted the transferred node with `Fr(~nodekey)`, which meant a former
    patron could only ever countersign a transfer for a node nobody else had
    heard of -- the interesting case, a signed transfer statement naming an
    EXISTING node, was outside the model.  Nothing on the wire stops a
    keyholder signing such a statement; what stops it mattering is the
    destination binding, which is what the lemma below tests.             */
rule Transfer_Countersign:
    [ !LtkC($Old, ~osk)                // the FORMER patron's key (classical)
    , In(nodekey)                      // ANY node it cares to name
    , !Pk($New, pkNew) ]               // a registered destination patron
  --[ CountersignedFor($Old, nodekey, $New) ]->
    [ Out(<'transfer', nodekey, $Old, $New,
           sign(<'transfer', nodekey, $Old, $New>, ~osk)>) ]

rule Patron_Accept_Transfer:
    [ !LtkC($New, ~nsk)                         // the new patron signs the result
    , !Pk($Old, pkOld)                          // the former patron's key
    , In(<'transfer', nodekey, $Old, $New, prov>) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(prov, <'transfer', nodekey, $Old, $New>, pkOld), true)
    , Distinct($Old, $New)                      // identical keys = no transfer
    , PatronAcceptedTransferEvidence($New, $Old, nodekey) ]->
    [ Out(sign(<'adopted', nodekey, $New>, ~nsk)) ]

rule Patron_Accept_Recovery:
    [ !LtkC($P, ~psk)                           // patron's own key (classical)
    , !PkC($S, pkOldC), !PkP($S, pkOldP)        // the old identity, both halves
    , !PkC($V, pkVC),   !PkP($V, pkVP)          // the verifier, both halves
    , In(<'rotate', $S, newkey, $P, <provAc, provAp>>)   // factor a (field 3)
    , In(<'recognise', $S, newkey, <provBc, provBp>>) ]  // factor b (field 9)
  --[ Eq(verify(provAc, <'rotate', $S, newkey, $P>, pkOldC), true)
    , Eq(verify(provAp, <'rotate', $S, newkey, $P>, pkOldP), true)
    , Eq(verify(provBc, <'recognise', $S, newkey>, pkVC), true)
    , Eq(verify(provBp, <'recognise', $S, newkey>, pkVP), true)
    , Distinct($V, $S)                          // the verifier is NOT the subject
    , Distinct($P, $S)                          // nor is the patron the subject
    , Distinct(pkOldC, newkey)                  // and the rotation is real

/*  WHAT THESE CHECKS ARE AND ARE NOT.  `wire-format.md` 4.1 requires two
    inequalities of a recovery adoption: "The node and the patron MUST
    differ", where the node is FIELD 1 -- the NEW key -- and "`prior_key`
    MUST differ from field 1".

    `Distinct(pkOld, newkey)` is the second of those, and it was missing: a
    "recovery" onto the key already held is no rotation, and nothing here
    rejected it.

    THE FIRST ONE CANNOT BE STATED IN THIS ABSTRACTION, and saying so is more
    useful than pretending otherwise.  This theory carries a node's identity
    as a stable public name `$S` with keys as separate terms, whereas RHTN
    identifies a node BY its keyhash -- which is exactly why a rotation
    changes identity and needs a successor statement at all.  So `newkey` and
    `$P` live in different domains here and cannot be compared, and
    `Distinct($P, $S)` is a check on the PRIOR identity, not on field 1.
    It was previously commented as though it were the wire's node/patron
    rule.  It is not, and the wire's rule is therefore unexercised: a
    validator that omitted it would not be caught by this theory.

    Closing that properly means carrying identities and keys in one keyhash
    domain throughout, which is a reshape of this theory rather than an added
    line.  Recorded rather than papered over.                              */
    , ReliedOn($P, $V, $S)                      // which verifier was trusted
    , PatronAcceptedRecoveryEvidence($P, $S, newkey) ]->
    [ Out(sign(<'recovered', $S, newkey>, ~psk)) ]  // the countersigned result

/*  WHAT THAT ACTION DOES NOT MEAN.  It marks the moment THIS PATRON judged the
    two recovery factors sufficient -- nothing wider.  A recovery adoption is a
    two-signer transaction (design Section 6.1: node and patron), and the
    successor's own envelope signature, the Adoption body, the signer set, and
    the rest of `wire-format.md` Section 4.1's structural checks are all
    OUTSIDE this theory.  So a trace reaching this action is NOT a trace in
    which a valid recovery transaction was accepted by the network; it is one
    in which a patron's evidence test passed.  The lemmas below inherit that
    scope exactly, which is why the action carries the patron in its first
    argument -- there is no observer-independent "accepted" here to name.  */

/*
  ONE REGISTRATION PER NAME.  A public label like $A stands for a keyhash,
  and design Section 5.1 makes a keyhash a commitment to the key material --
  change the material and you have a different identity.  Without the
  restriction below, Register could fire twice for one label and give a
  single symbolic name two unrelated keys, so "the party I meant to reach"
  would not pin a key.  (currency.spthy's Subject_Key is deliberately NOT
  restricted this way: a subject holding several keys over time is rotation,
  which is that theory's subject matter.)
*/
restriction OneKeyPerName:
  "All a #i #j. OnlyOnce(a) @ #i & OnlyOnce(a) @ #j ==> #i = #j"

restriction Equality:
  "All x y #i. Eq(x, y) @ #i ==> x = y"

/*
  THE SUBJECT IS NEVER ITS OWN VERIFIER (wire Section 5.3: "The current
  counterparty is never a candidate for their own verification").  Without
  this, a thief holding ONLY the subject's stolen key satisfies factor a
  (the old-key proof) AND forges factor b by signing a recognition under
  that same stolen key -- self-recognition.  The FIRST version of this
  model omitted the rule and Tamarin found exactly that trace, which is why
  the rule is called out here: wire Section 5.3's distinctness requirement
  is load-bearing for recovery, not only for ordinary presence.  The
  Distinct restriction discards every trace where the recognising verifier
  is the subject.
*/
restriction VerifierIsNotSubject:
  "All V S #i. Distinct(V, S) @ #i ==> not(V = S)"

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  LEMMAS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*/

// SANITY: an honest recovery completes -- old-key proof plus honest
// recognition plus patron acceptance.  Guards against a vacuous security
// lemma.
// AND THE HONEST ONE, excluding compromise -- see attach.spthy for why an
// executability lemma that permits it guards nothing.
/*  WHAT HYBRIDISING FIELD 9 BUYS.  `wire-format.md`: "field 9 forges a
    VERIFIER'S attestation, which is the attack", and its protection "is
    permanent" -- a forged classical `match` would otherwise "sit inside an
    authentic post-quantum record indefinitely".  So an attacker holding only
    the verifier's classical half cannot put that verifier's recognition on an
    accepted recovery.  Delete either `verify(provB...)` check and this
    falsifies.                                                              */
lemma forging_a_recognition_needs_both_verifier_halves:
  "All P S newkey V #i.
     PatronAcceptedRecoveryEvidence(P, S, newkey) @ #i
     & ReliedOn(P, V, S) @ #i
     & not(Ex #k. CompromisedPQ(V) @ #k)
     ==> Ex #b. RecognisedFor(V, S, newkey) @ #b & #b < #i"

/*  THIS MUST TIE THE MEETING TO THE KEY THAT WAS RECOVERED, and an earlier
    version did not.  It asked only for SOME meeting before SOME acceptance,
    which `Recognise_Without_Meeting` satisfies trivially: hold a meeting with
    one verifier about one key, then recover a different key on a different
    verifier's unearned recognition, with nothing compromised.  The honest
    path could have been deleted and this would still have verified.

    `HonestlyRecognised` is emitted only by the rule that consumes
    `AtMeeting`, and the meeting, the recognition, the verifier relied upon
    and the accepted successor are now the SAME (V, S, newkey).            */
lemma recovery_completes_honestly:
  exists-trace
  "Ex P S newkey V #m #r #i.
       MeetingKey(V, S, newkey) @ #m
     & HonestlyRecognised(V, S, newkey) @ #r
     & ReliedOn(P, V, S) @ #i
     & PatronAcceptedRecoveryEvidence(P, S, newkey) @ #i
     & #m < #r & #r < #i
     & not(Ex A #k. Compromised(A) @ #k)"

lemma recovery_is_executable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex P S newkey #i. PatronAcceptedRecoveryEvidence(P, S, newkey) @ #i"

// THE TWO-FACTOR PROPERTY (Stage 1.1).  wire Section 4.1 states it: "Key
// alone is not recovery" and "presence alone is not recovery".  Rather than one lemma asserting both
// factors (which a thief with a stolen key subtly evades -- see below), we
// prove the two MIRROR IMPOSSIBILITIES: neither factor by itself is ever
// enough.  Together they are exactly the two-factor requirement.
//
// WHY NOT ONE COMBINED LEMMA.  A first version demanded the honest
// OldKeyProof ACTION for factor a.  Tamarin falsified it with a revealing
// trace: a thief who has stolen the subject's key produces the rotate
// proof by ADVERSARY signing (the stolen key speaks), so the honest rule
// never fires -- yet factor a is genuinely satisfied, because "the old key
// authorised this" is true however the key was held.  The trace also
// showed the model's boundary plainly: with the key stolen AND an honest
// verifier recognising IDENTITY S, recovery succeeds -- because the
// symbolic model cannot represent the verifier checking a FACE.  That is
// design Section 18.3's colluding/deceived-counterparty residual, not a
// break of the two-factor structure.  The two lemmas below sidestep the
// artifact by asking the sound question: is EITHER factor alone enough?

// FACTOR A IS NECESSARY: an honest recognition with no key authorisation
// never recovers.  A verifier vouching for the person is not enough on its
// own; without the old key signing the rotation (OldKeyProof by the honest
// holder, OR the key having been stolen so the adversary can sign), and
// with no patron compromise, no recovery is accepted.  "presence alone is
// not recovery" (design Section 9.1).
lemma recognition_alone_insufficient:
  "not( Ex P S newkey #i.
          PatronAcceptedRecoveryEvidence(P, S, newkey) @ #i
        & (Ex V #b. Recognised(V, S) @ #b)
        & not(Ex #a. OldKeyProof(S, newkey) @ #a)   // no honest key proof
        & not(Ex #c. Compromised(S) @ #c)           // and no stolen key
        & not(Ex #kp. Compromised(P) @ #kp) )"       // and no patron compromise

// THE SUCCESSOR BINDING IS LOAD-BEARING.  Every accepted recovery rests on a
// recognition NAMING THAT successor key.  This is the model's check on wire
// Section 4.1's rule that a verifier response must name the newly adopted
// node and the prior key; drop the binding from Recognise above and this
// lemma is falsified, which is how we know it is doing work.
lemma recognition_binds_the_successor:
  "All P S newkey #i.
     PatronAcceptedRecoveryEvidence(P, S, newkey) @ #i
     ==> ( (Ex V #b. RecognisedFor(V, S, newkey) @ #b & #b < #i)
         | (Ex V #kv. ReliedOn(P, V, S) @ #i
                    & Compromised(V) @ #kv & #kv < #i)
         | (Ex #kp. Compromised(P) @ #kp & #kp < #i) )"

// THE PATRON BINDING IS LOAD-BEARING TOO.  Every accepted recovery rests on
// an old-key proof naming THE ACCEPTING PATRON.  Drop the third element of
// the successor statement and this falsifies with one proof accepted by two
// patrons -- wire Section 4.1's reason for putting patron_key in it.
lemma successor_statement_binds_the_patron:
  "All P S newkey #i.
     PatronAcceptedRecoveryEvidence(P, S, newkey) @ #i
     ==> ( (Ex #a. OldKeyProofFor(S, newkey, P) @ #a & #a < #i)
         | (Ex #c. Compromised(S) @ #c & #c < #i)
         | (Ex #kp. Compromised(P) @ #kp & #kp < #i) )"

// THE TRANSFER STATEMENT BINDS ITS DESTINATION.  Every accepted transfer rests
// on a countersignature naming THIS new patron -- so one countersignature does
// not authorise moves its signer never approved.  Drop $New from the signed
// payload and this falsifies, exactly as successor_statement_binds_the_patron
// does for the recovery route.
lemma transfer_statement_binds_the_destination:
  "All New Old nodekey #i.
     PatronAcceptedTransferEvidence(New, Old, nodekey) @ #i
     ==> ( (Ex #a. CountersignedFor(Old, nodekey, New) @ #a & #a < #i)
         | (Ex #k. Compromised(Old) @ #k & #k < #i) )"

end
